WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.102
Now we can assemble a simplified encoder layer from familiar parts.

00:00:04.102 --> 00:00:07.402
Start with x, a batch of token vectors.

00:00:07.402 --> 00:00:10.552
Pass x through multi-head self-attention.

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:14.772
Add the attention output back to x and normalize the result.

00:00:14.772 --> 00:00:19.212
Next, pass the normalized vectors through the feed-forward network.

00:00:19.212 --> 00:00:24.897
Add that feed-forward output back to the previous representation and normalize again.

00:00:24.897 --> 00:00:31.817
The result has the same basic shape as the original input: one updated hidden vector for every token.

00:00:31.817 --> 00:00:40.412
That shape can be passed directly into another encoder layer, which is how Transformers become deep stacks.

00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:45.172
When reading the code, do not treat each line as an isolated command.

00:00:45.172 --> 00:00:52.267
Follow the data path: x, attention, residual plus normalization, feed-forward, residual plus normalization.

00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:57.577
Once you can trace that path, the architecture diagram and the Python implementation become two views of the

00:00:57.577 --> 00:00:59.909
same process.
